.docname {Challenges}
.include {docs}

Going from a walk to a clean model meets problems at every step: in the sensor, in the map it
builds, in the room itself, and in moving the data around. This page lists them, with what each
one costs and how vCortex lives with it.

![What the lidar sees from the floor](images/side-view.svg "What the lidar sees, seen from the side.")

## The sensor looks down

The robot is a Unitree Go2, a four-legged robot dog. Its lidar sits in its head and was built for
walking, not for mapping:

- it is mounted **upside down**, about **32 cm** above the floor;
- it is **tilted by about 15°**, so it looks mostly at the ground in front of the robot.

That is good for not tripping over things, and bad for seeing a room.

| Problem | Real numbers | What it costs |
|---|---|---|
| The ceiling is never seen | In one map, 99% of the dots lie less than 1.4 m above the floor | The room has no top |
| The top of the walls is barely seen | Only 2,518 of 811,074 points (0.31%) lie more than 1.10 m above the floor | **The wall height cannot be measured: you type it in** (2.70 m by default) |
| High things look short | A wardrobe is seen up to where the laser stops reaching | An object's top is "the highest point seen", not its real top |
| Every distance reads a bit too long | +11.3 cm on every reading, before calibration | Fixed on the robot, before the map reaches vCortex |

An old idea was to keep only the dots high up, where furniture ends and only walls remain. With
this sensor it cannot work: between 1.1 m and 2.5 m above the floor a whole room left only 112
small squares of 5 cm with any dot in them, and no straight line longer than 1.05 m.

## The map is not straight

| Problem | Real numbers | What it costs |
|---|---|---|
| The floor in the map is tilted | 0.86° in one map, 2.4° in the map shown in the Overview; 0.86° is already **11 cm** of height across a 7.6 m room | Every height is measured from the floor right under it, not from one fixed level |
| The map is turned at a random angle | It follows the way the robot faced at the start: 16.8° in the map shown in the Overview | vCortex turns the map so the walls run along the axes, then turns the result back |
| Stray dots float in the air | A few dots far from everything else | They are dropped before anything else happens |

## People and moving things

A person walking past the robot is measured like anything else. Because they keep moving, they
leave a thin trail of dots across the room.

- **Counting dots does not help.** The map keeps at most one dot per 5 cm cube, so a wall and a
  passing person can look the same. In one test map, 57.3% of the cubes held fewer than 3 dots.
- **What would help** is counting how many turns of the laser saw each spot: a wall is seen again
  and again, a person only for a moment. vCortex does not do this yet, so a passer-by can show up
  as a small object.

## Walls and furniture look alike

To the laser, a wall and a wardrobe are both "a flat, upright surface". Telling them apart is the
hardest part of vCortex.

- **Furniture hides walls.** A wardrobe against a wall covers it from top to bottom. A row of desks
  along a wall can hide metres of it.
- **The longest line is not always a wall.** In one room the longest, densest straight lines (3.0
  to 3.5 m) were a row of furniture in the middle. The real walls were thinner and sparser,
  because the laser saw them from far away.
- **Some walls are barely there.** A wall seen only at a sharp angle, or a glass wall the laser
  passes through, leaves very few dots, sometimes only at its base, under the desks.

So vCortex cannot pick walls by height, length or density alone. It also looks at where the robot
walked and at what lies behind each line. The [Algorithm](algorithm.qd) section explains how.

## Getting the data off the robot

| Problem | Why | What vCortex does |
|---|---|---|
| The robot's computer is busy | It is already building the map in real time | vCortex runs on a laptop, not on the robot |
| The robot's own way of sharing data does not suit Wi-Fi | It finds peers by shouting to the whole network, which Wi-Fi often blocks, and it cuts big messages into many small pieces: a whole map is about 0.8 MB | A small program on the robot sends the map over one ordinary connection |
| The robot runs old software | Its robot software stack reached end of life in 2023 | vCortex needs none of it, so anyone can run it |

## Limits today

What vCortex does not handle yet, as a result of the problems above:

.box {Not handled yet} type:{warning}
    - **Wall height is typed in.** The lidar never sees the top of the walls. The default is 2.70 m.
    - **Right angles only.** Walls at other angles or curved walls are not found.
    - **Objects are boxes.** Each object is a box around what was seen. It has no name yet, and its
      top is the highest point the lidar reached, which may be lower than the real top.
    - **One floor, one room tested.** The test maps are single rooms. Walls shared by two rooms
      are not tested yet.
    - **No doors or windows.** A gap in a wall only marks the room as open.
    - **People leave traces.** Someone walking past the robot can show up as a small object.
    - **One robot at a time.** A second robot replaces the first; their maps are not merged.

Possible ways forward, one per limit. None of them is built; they are ideas to try.

| Limit | A possible way forward |
|---|---|
| Wall height is typed in | Tilt the lidar up for a short pass, or add a second sensor that looks up, and read the ceiling. Or take the height from a plan of the building |
| Right angles only | Find the main directions of each room on its own, instead of one angle for the whole map. Curved walls would need curves, not straight sides |
| Objects are boxes | Name each box by its size and height (a desk is about 0.75 m high, a wardrobe about 2 m), or with a trained classifier on its dots. Turn each box to fit its own dots, not the walls |
| One room tested | Survey buildings with several rooms and corridors, and add them to the test maps with measured ground truth |
| No doors or windows | A gap of 0.7 to 1.2 m in a wall, from floor up, is likely a door. A window is a hole in the wall dots at desk height and above |
| People leave traces | Use `num_scan_seen`, the number of scans that saw each cube. Something seen in only a few scans, while the robot stood near it many times, was moving |
| One robot at a time | Line the maps of the robots up in one frame first, then let them all feed one map store. The model already works on any set of dots and poses |
